WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.300
Did the rules change regarding this Mandalorian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:07.680
tool in the season 3 finale? Or is there&nbsp;
an explanation hidden in plain sight?

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:12.240
There have been many major battles across&nbsp;
"The Mandalorian" and its multiple seasons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:17.880
but few of them can match the scale and intensity&nbsp;
of the battle of Mandalore in the Season 3 finale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:23.280
episode. As Din Djarin and Grogu have their&nbsp;
climactic confrontation with Moff Gideon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:28.320
so too do Din's fellow Mandalorians engage&nbsp;
in their own big fight to retake their home&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:33.060
planet once and for all. It all makes for&nbsp;
another epic "Star Wars" battle, aside from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:38.392
one tiny problem — one part of the on-screen&nbsp;
skirmish doesn't really make sense to some fans.

00:00:38.534 --> 00:00:43.380
Throughout the battle, audiences see various&nbsp;
characters — particularly Axe Woves and his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:48.540
fellow Mandalorian fighters — using jetpacks for&nbsp;
traversal. This isn't anything new for the show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:53.580
as a previous episode of "The Mandalorian" also&nbsp;
saw characters employing jetpacks. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:58.620
the exact manner in which this piece of gear is&nbsp;
used in the finale may conflict with an important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:02.880
bit of information that may have been established&nbsp;
in the series just a few episodes prior.

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:08.880
During the Season 3 finale of "The Mandalorian,"&nbsp;
Axe Woves uses a jetpack to manually fly into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:13.260
space and, later, propel himself back&nbsp;
to Mandalore's surface. Considering the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:17.100
show's sci-fi trappings, there's not much of&nbsp;
an issue with that plot point — or, at least,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:21.120
there wouldn't be if it didn't seem to&nbsp;
violate the in-universe rules that "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:24.420
Mandalorian" had set for itself&nbsp;
earlier in the very same season.

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:29.880
Back in Season 3, Episode 4 of "The Mandalorian,"&nbsp;
it seems to be established that jetpacks in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:33.600
"Star Wars" universe can only be used&nbsp;
to carry their users a relatively short&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:37.440
distance. If that's true, it makes sense&nbsp;
to add such a limitation to what would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:41.640
otherwise be a pretty overpowered piece&nbsp;
of gear, especially as the story required&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:45.600
a reason that the group couldn't simply&nbsp;
pursue their prey with jetpacks. Granted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:49.020
it seemed like the bigger problem was&nbsp;
the noise that the jetpacks would make.

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:53.880
"The mountains are too high. If we&nbsp;
use jetpacks, the beast will hear."

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:58.740
Either way, they didn't clarify exactly&nbsp;
how far the jetpacks could take them if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:02.280
fully fueled up. The problem for&nbsp;
some fans is that this seeming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:06.480
limitation is tossed out the window in&nbsp;
the Season 3 finale. According to them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:11.520
Axe Woves shouldn't be able to take himself all&nbsp;
the way up into space with a "Star Wars" jetpack,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:16.155
let alone still have the fuel to fly out of&nbsp;
the ship before it crashes to the surface.

00:02:16.224 --> 00:02:19.860
All in all, if it is a continuity&nbsp;
error, it's a fairly minor one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:23.734
that doesn't really affect the overall&nbsp;
quality of the battle or the episode.

00:02:23.734 --> 00:02:26.220
Some diehard fans of "The Mandalorian" also took&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:29.640
notice of the apparent discrepancy in&nbsp;
the use of jetpacks during the Season&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:33.805
3 finale. As one Reddit user put it in&nbsp;
their list of issues with the episode:

00:02:33.805 --> 00:02:37.500
"The jetpacks limitation was a pretty big&nbsp;
issue in a previous foundling episode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:40.380
but now someone can just hit&nbsp;
low orbit with no problem."

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:45.120
Nonetheless, one fan has pushed back on&nbsp;
the critique by offering a theory that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:49.200
could possibly explain why the jetpacks in&nbsp;
the Season 3 finale seem to be so much more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:54.060
powerful compared to the ones featured in&nbsp;
Season 3, Episode 4. The fan suggests that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:58.440
there might be a difference in build quality&nbsp;
between the jetpacks used by the Children of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:02.820
the Watch and the ones used by Axe Woves&nbsp;
and his fellow Mandalorians. They wrote,

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:06.660
"I'm thinking the Children of the Watch group&nbsp;
were making do with whatever they could find,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:09.901
building their equipment in&nbsp;
a cave with a box of scraps."

00:03:09.901 --> 00:03:15.240
By contrast, the Mandalorian resistance had access&nbsp;
to regular society and would naturally be able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:19.980
take advantage of better tools and equipment.&nbsp;
Another answer could be that they simply had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:24.840
more fuel prepared for the battle in contrast to&nbsp;
getting taken unprepared by the raptor before they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:29.820
could top off the tank. Neither theory has been&nbsp;
explicitly confirmed, leaving fans to make up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.820 --> 00:03:35.100
their own minds about whether this is a continuity&nbsp;
error or simply a subtle bit of storytelling.

